    "content": "An AMBER Alert is an emergency child abduction broadcast system that quickly notifies the public to help locate a missing child believed to be in imminent danger. It uses radio, TV, highway signs, and wireless devices to blast information about the child and suspect across a designated area.Key Aspects of AMBER AlertsCriteria: Generally, law enforcement must believe a child (under 18) has been abducted, is in danger of serious bodily harm or death, and there is enough descriptive information to help the public find them.Distribution: Alerts are sent directly to cell phones via the Wireless Emergency Alerts program.Impact: Since its inception in 1996, the system has helped safely recover over 1,290 children in the U.S..Origin: Named for 9-year-old Amber Hagerman, who was abducted and murdered in Texas in 1996.If you receive an AMBER Alert on your phone or see one on a highway sign, take the information seriously and check the official AMBER Alert website for updates.",
